1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
What if your broadcast network could fix
its own ad revenue leaks?

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,000
So, I'll block ad reporting for our Roku
viewers.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
The video keeps playing. Skipping the
alert wait, watch what AdBreak catches.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,000
There it is. Missing impressions, isolated
to Roku.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:27,875
The ad played, but its billing report
never arrived. A healthy stream can still

6
00:00:27,875 --> 00:00:29,000
lose money.

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,789
AdBreak checks the ad server and delivery
metrics through Grafana MCP,

8
00:00:34,789 --> 00:00:39,000
then tries to disprove its diagnosis
before acting.

9
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:47,125
The checks point to reporting. It selects
the approved runbook and switches Roku

10
00:00:47,125 --> 00:00:49,000
to server-side reporting.

11
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:56,059
And after the next complete ad break,
impressions are coming through again.

12
00:00:56,059 --> 00:00:59,000
The stream kept playing throughout.

13
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:04,238
That is AdBreak: an autonomous revenue SRE
for broadcast operations,

14
00:01:04,238 --> 00:01:10,000
built with Gemini on Vertex AI and
Google's Agent Development Kit.

15
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,739
This is our simulated broadcast plant. The
live channel takes device,

16
00:01:15,739 --> 00:01:22,000
region, and CDN parameters, while 200
synthetic viewers exercise the ad
pipeline.

17
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:28,087
This run repaired the fault in 78 seconds
and verified recovery in about four

18
00:01:28,087 --> 00:01:32,000
minutes twenty, for roughly two cents in
model cost.

19
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Now, I'll make the ad server stop
returning ads.

20
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:43,500
AdBreak finds the no-fill problem and
prepares a fallback.

21
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:49,000
But this change would replace inventory
across the channel.

22
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:58,375
So it stops here. The plan is ready, but
nothing executes until an operator
approves

23
00:01:58,375 --> 00:01:59,000
it.

24
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Approval rechecks the live conditions
first. If the situation has changed while

25
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,000
the operator was away, the old plan
doesn't get a free pass.

26
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Now, what happens if the agent itself gets
stuck?

27
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,150
Here, we've forced repeated tool calls.
Its watchdog catches the loop and stops

28
00:02:22,150 --> 00:02:26,000
the run, keeping the evidence it
collected.

29
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:32,286
Grafana watches the agent too: its
latency, model spend, and watchdog
interventions.

30
00:02:32,286 --> 00:02:37,000
The operator can inspect the system doing
the repairs.

31
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,696
After a successful repair, AdBreak writes
the incident report.

32
00:02:41,696 --> 00:02:49,000
The next operator can see what failed,
what changed, and how recovery was
verified.

33
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:55,000
You can inspect the incident traces and
try the live channel at the project links.

34
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Keep the stream playing. Keep the ad
revenue accounted for.
